# RFP Hub publisher dashboard — local environment
#
# Copy this to `packages/dashboard/.env.local`, which Next reads automatically and which is
# gitignored:
#
#   cp .env-example .env.local
#   pnpm --filter @the-rfp-hub/dashboard dev
#
# BOTH VARIABLES ARE `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, WHICH MEANS TWO THINGS.
#
#   1. They are INLINED INTO THE BUNDLE AT BUILD TIME. Changing them on a running host does
#      nothing; the deployment has to be rebuilt. The dashboard says so on screen when one is
#      missing, because this is the single most common way to lose an afternoon here.
#   2. They are PUBLIC. Anyone who loads the page can read them. Neither is a secret — an API
#      origin and an auth application id are both identifiers — and NOTHING SECRET MAY EVER BE
#      ADDED WITH THIS PREFIX. The dashboard holds no server-side credential at all: every
#      authenticated request is made by the browser with the signed-in user's own access token.

# Where the API lives. No trailing slash needed (one is trimmed). This origin is also written into
# the page's Content-Security-Policy `connect-src`, so the browser may talk to this API and to
# nothing else — which is why a deployment pointing at a different API must be rebuilt rather than
# reconfigured.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3004

# The auth application this environment logs in against.
#
# USE A SEPARATE APPLICATION PER ENVIRONMENT — development, staging and production each get their
# own, and all of them separate from any other product's. The application is the user pool: sharing
# one means a test login in development is a real account in production's directory, and it means
# the API's token check (`aud` must equal its own application id) cannot tell the environments
# apart. The API's `PRIVY_APP_ID` must name the SAME application this does.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID=
